### Custom ###
# The compose stack reads /.env at the repository root -- POSTGRES_PASSWORD,
# REDIS_PASSWORD and AES_KEY are in it. /backend/.env below is where it used to
# live; the rule outlived the file and the root one was matched by nothing, so a
# `git add .` staged every credential the stack has.
/.env
!/.env.example
/backend/.env
/backend/src/main/resources/application.yaml

### Mounted secrets (the real files behind /secrets in the compose stack) ###
# db.properties carries the database URL, user and password; application.properties
# the backend's own. Production keeps both in the cooknco-config Secret, and
# letsencrypt/ holds issued certificates and their private keys.
/secrets/

### Firebase credentials (push notifications) ###
# The service account key can send a notification to every install of the app.
# Production keeps it in the cooknco-config Secret; see docs/notifications.md.
/config/fcm-service-account.json
#
# app/androidApp/google-services.json is deliberately NOT ignored. The Google Services
# plugin turns it into string resources that ship inside the APK — google_api_key and
# gcm_defaultSenderId are in resources.arsc of every build — so every install already
# carries it, and Firebase's security comes from Security Rules and App Check rather
# than from hiding it. Tracking it is what lets a clean clone build the app.

### Kubernetes migration artefacts (contain database dumps and user uploads) ###
/k8s/migration/backup-*/
/k8s/migration/.last-backup
/k8s/migration/.migration-plan
/k8s/migration/.migration-plan.*

# Kotlin compiler scratch (session locks, error logs) - regenerated every build
.kotlin/

### Capacitor Android build output ###
# `npx cap add/sync android` regenerates the whole tree, local.properties included,
# and it carries a copy of the built frontend bundle with it.
/frontend/android/
